/T (Through their collaborative work, Karen and Erica bring together Indigenous and Western ways of knowing, being, and doing, showing how research can be shaped through respect, relationship-building, and shared learning. Together, their work reflects Etuaptmumk in practice by creating space for different knowledge systems to inform research, teaching, and community-engaged collaboration. )
/E (Through their collaborative work, Karen and Erica bring together Indigenous and Western ways of knowing, being, and doing, showing how research can be shaped through respect, relationship-building, and shared learning. Together, their work reflects Etuaptmumk in practice by creating space for different knowledge systems to inform research, teaching, and community-engaged collaboration. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
315 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 211 0 R
/T (While the two first met at high school in Corner Brook, NL, Karen and Erica have recently partnered to establish the Transforming Relations and Convergent Knowledges \(TRACK\) Co-Lab. TRACK is a research collective that provides space and place for members to pursue research pathways that transform current practices in Indigenous and western scientific knowledge systems. )
/E (While the two first met at high school in Corner Brook, NL, Karen and Erica have recently partnered to establish the Transforming Relations and Convergent Knowledges \(TRACK\) Co-Lab. TRACK is a research collective that provides space and place for members to pursue research pathways that transform current practices in Indigenous and western scientific knowledge systems. )
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
